    "content": "In 1927, Mahatma Gandhi was convalescing in Bangalore. Thanthai Periyar, who was spearheading the radical Self-Respect Movement across the Madras Presidency, traveled to meet him alongside his close colleague S. Ramanathan.\n\nBoth titans sat cross-legged on the floor. Gandhi wore his characteristic khadi loincloth with an enigmatic, gentle smile; Periyar possessed the burning gaze of an uncompromising social iconoclast.\n\nThe conversation quickly moved past polite pleasantries to the deepest cancer of Indian society: the caste system and religious orthodoxy.\n\nPeriyar laid down his challenge with characteristic bluntness:\n\"Gandhiji! You travel across the country defending the Varna system. But is not the very bedrock of Hinduism built upon the graded birth inequality of Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra, and the untouchable Panchama? Unless this religious architecture that denies human dignity is uprooted, genuine social emancipation is an illusion!\"\n\nMahatma Gandhi adjusted his spectacles and replied in his soft, measured tone:\n\"Ramasami! You are looking at the corrupted, degraded practice of Varnashrama. In true Varnashrama, there is no high and low. The duty of a scavenger and the duty of a doctor are of equal moral worth to society. Division of labor is not division of human souls. We do not need to destroy religion; we can reform it from within!\"\n\nPeriyar immediately countered with an unassailable empirical argument:\n\"What you describe is an idealized dream, Gandhiji—it exists only in poetic imagination! What is the ground reality for thousands of years? Millions of human beings are treated worse than cattle, denied literacy, barred from public roads, and forbidden from temples!\nSuppose you have a vessel filled with poisoned milk. You say: 'Let us wash the vessel and filter the milk.' I say: 'Pour out the deadly poison and smash the vessel, so that no one drinks poison again!' You speak from scriptures; I speak from the agony of the streets!\"\n\nGandhi remained silent for a long moment. He recognized that their philosophical paths were fundamentally distinct: Periyar was a thoroughgoing rationalist revolutionary, while Gandhi was an ethical religious reformer.\n\nThe intense debate continued for hours. Neither persuaded the other to abandon his core worldview. Yet, there was not a single drop of malice or personal rancor in the room.\n\nAs they rose to bid farewell, Gandhi grasped Periyar's hands with immense warmth and said:\n\"Ramasami! We do not agree today. But I recognize your absolute honesty and deep love for our suffering people. We shall meet again and continue our dialogue!\"\n\nPeriyar smiled and bowed with equal respect. Two ideological opposites had demonstrated how two towering figures could wage fierce intellectual battle while preserving profound personal affection and democratic grace.",
