    "content": "On September 22, 1931, in the working-class neighborhood of Canning Town in East London, two of the most recognizable men on Earth met in the modest home of Dr. Katial. On one side stood Mahatma Gandhi, leader of India's non-violent independence struggle; on the other stood Charlie Chaplin, the legendary silent cinema master whose 'Little Tramp' embodied the dignity of the common working man.\n\nChaplin had long admired Gandhi's moral courage. Yet one burning question puzzled the cinematic artist: Why did Gandhi oppose industrialization and modern machinery so vehemently?\n\nAs they sat down together, Chaplin broached the subject with genuine curiosity:\n\"Mr. Gandhi, why are you so opposed to modern machinery? Doesn't machinery emancipate human beings from backbreaking drudgery? Doesn't it offer mankind leisure, efficiency, and material abundance?\"\n\nGandhi looked at Chaplin with a twinkle in his eyes and smiled softly:\n\"My dear Chaplin! The world has misunderstood my stance. I am not against machinery in the abstract. But look at India.\nWe are a nation of three hundred million people. For six months of the year, our agricultural peasantry has no work. \nThe vast mechanical textile mills of Manchester destroyed the spinning wheels of our villages. A single great steam loom can do the work of a hundred villagers in an instant. The mill owner in London gets enormously wealthy; but what becomes of the hundred Indian families who lose their livelihood? They starve! They are reduced to beggary!\"\n\nChaplin listened intently to Gandhi's socio-economic logic. He then pressed further:\n\"Gandhiji! If working hours were reduced, if workers were guaranteed adequate leisure, and if the wealth generated by machines were equitably shared among all who work, would you then support machinery?\"\n\nGandhi's eyes lit up as he replied emphatically:\n\"Most certainly! The machine must be the servant of man, never his master. When a machine concentrates wealth in the hands of a few and creates widespread unemployment, it is an instrument of violence. When it relieves human misery and uplifts the community equally, it is a blessing!\"\n\nChaplin was deeply moved. The conversation crystallized the themes that had long troubled the filmmaker's conscience. \n\nFive years later, in 1936, Chaplin released his crowning masterpiece, **Modern Times**, depicting the Little Tramp swallowed whole by the massive turning gears of a factory assembly line. When audiences across the globe laughed and cried at Chaplin's biting satire of industrial exploitation, they were witnessing the artistic embodiment of the very truth Gandhi had articulated in that London parlor: \"Is the machine for man, or is man for the machine?\"",
