    "source_context": "Gandhi's delightful conversation with ashram children complaining about his concise answers compared to Lord Krishna's eighteen chapters in the Gita.",
    "summary": "When children asked why he answered in single sentences while Krishna gave an entire chapter to Arjuna, Gandhi laughed: 'Krishna had only one Arjuna; I face a whole battalion of you!'",
    "reading_time": "5 min",
    "witty_quote": "Krishna had only one Arjuna to deal with. Here in Sabarmati, I face an entire army of Arjunas! Show some mercy to your old grandfather!",
    "reflection": "Treating children as intellectual equals, Gandhi demonstrated how sacred epics can be made vibrant and humorous in everyday moral education.",
    "content": "On the banks of the Sabarmati River in Gujarat, Mahatma Gandhi's ashram was home to dozens of children and orphans who grew up under his affectionate care.\n\nEvery evening after prayers, Gandhi gathered the children to discuss the Bhagavad Gita, the Upanishads, and moral parables.\n\nOne evening, a group of mischievous boys and girls surrounded Gandhi with mock indignation on their faces.\n\nA young boy raised his hand and voiced their collective grievance:\n\"Bapu! We have a serious complaint against you!\nIn the Mahabharata, Arjuna asked Lord Krishna a single brief question on the battlefield, and Krishna responded with an entire, magnificent chapter!\nYet, when we write you long, detailed questions on a full sheet of paper, you dismiss us with a single line: 'Speak the truth' or 'Do your chores!' Is this fair, Bapu? Why don't you answer us at length like Lord Krishna?\"\n\nThe ashram elders held their breath, wondering how the Mahatma would handle the insolent inquiry.\n\nGandhi took off his round spectacles, placed them on his lap, and threw back his head in joyous laughter.\n\nPulling the boy onto his knee, Gandhi replied with warmth:\n\"My dear children! Your grievance sounds very convincing. But you have overlooked one crucial difference!\nOn the battlefield of Kurukshetra, Lord Krishna had to deal with only *one* Arjuna! Therefore, he could afford to spend eighteen chapters answering his doubts.\nLook at me here in Sabarmati! Do I have just one Arjuna? No! An entire battalion of sharp, inquisitive Arjunas is interrogating me every single hour! If I gave each of you eighteen chapters, I would be talking until doomsday! Have some mercy on your old Bapu!\"\n\nThe children clapped their hands and burst into delighted laughter.\n\nGandhi then added gently: \"Remember, practicing a single small virtue in daily life is worth more than listening to a thousand lengthy sermons.\"\n\nBy stepping down to the children's level with wit and affection, Gandhi taught that true spiritual wisdom is rooted in simple, practical action.",
