    "id": "debate-115-ambedkar-anup-singh-temple-demon-1955",
    "slug": "debate-115-ambedkar-anup-singh-temple-demon-1955",
    "title": "Ambedkar and Anup Singh: When the Demon Enters the Temple — 1955",
    "source_author": "Dr. B. R. Ambedkar & Dr. Anup Singh",
    "source_year": "1955",
    "source_book": "Rajya Sabha Official Debates (Vol. 9, September 1955) & Ambedkar Writings and Speeches",
    "source_context": "Dr. B. R. Ambedkar's fierce, unforgettable debate in the Rajya Sabha defending his controversial statement that he would not hesitate to burn the Constitution if it failed the oppressed.",
    "summary": "When challenged on why he once said he would burn his own Constitution, Ambedkar electrified the house: 'We built a temple for God to reside; but if a demon enters before God is installed, what choice is there but to tear down the temple?'",
    "reading_time": "5 min",
    "witty_quote": "We constructed a beautiful temple for God to dwell in. But if before God can be installed, a demon takes possession of the sanctum sanctorum, what can one do but destroy the temple?",
    "reflection": "Constitutions and laws are not idols for blind worship; their legitimacy exists solely in their capacity to deliver justice and equality to the weakest citizens.",
    "content": "In September 1955, the Rajya Sabha was engrossed in a fierce constitutional debate. Dr. Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar, seated on the opposition benches, was scrutinizing the central government's implementation of social justice safeguards.\n\nCongress member Dr. Anup Singh rose to challenge the Chief Architect of the Constitution:\n\"Mr. Chairman, Sir! The distinguished Dr. Ambedkar, who earned eternal glory by drafting our Constitution, made an astonishing statement in this very house some time ago. He declared: 'I shall be the first person to burn this Constitution!'\nWould a father burn his own child? Will Dr. Ambedkar explain to this nation why he uttered such a shocking threat against the sacred document he himself created?\"\n\nA heavy silence descended upon the chamber. Members leaned forward, anticipating an explosive confrontation.\n\nDr. Ambedkar rose with deliberate majesty. His dark eyes flashed with intellectual fire:\n\"Mr. Chairman, Sir! I am grateful to my friend Dr. Anup Singh for raising this question. On that earlier occasion, I did not have the time to explain my reasons. Today, I shall provide the explanation through a simple parable.\"\n\nThe house listened in rapt attention.\n\nAmbedkar's deep voice boomed through the hall:\n\"Friends! Suppose our people labor together to construct a magnificent temple so that God may reside within it and bless the poor.\nWe build the walls, the pillars, and the sanctum sanctorum. But before we can formally consecrate and install the idol of God, an evil demon slips inside in the dark of night and takes permanent possession of the temple!\nWhat are we to do then? If we lack the strength to drive the demon out, what alternative remains but to demolish the temple itself rather than allow a demon to be worshipped in the name of God?\"\n\nMember P. K. Sinha interjected: \"Dr. Ambedkar, why destroy the temple? Why not simply destroy the demon?\"\n\nAmbedkar fired back with blistering speed:\n\"Because we do not have the moral courage or the strength to destroy the demon! That is the tragic curse of Indian society!\nIf those who capture power use this Constitution not to install the God of Equality, Liberty, and Fraternity, but to entrench the demon of caste privilege and oligarchy, then this Constitution ceases to be a sacred temple. It becomes an instrument of oppression. And for the oppressed, burning such an instrument is the only path to self-respect!\"\n\nThe Rajya Sabha erupted in stunned murmurs and thunderous applause.\n\nAmbedkar's words were a revolutionary declaration: a nation's constitution is not an unalterable religious scripture to be blindly worshipped, but a living instrument whose sacredness depends entirely on whether it delivers justice to the marginalized.",
